WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.490
Picture a theatre's box office, the little window where you buy a ticket.

2
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.509
Behind it runs a small program.

3
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.581
Before the doors open, it sets up its parts.

4
00:00:12.581 --> 00:00:14.369
A seating plan of the hall.

5
00:00:14.369 --> 00:00:15.230
A price list.

6
00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.091
A sales book.

7
00:00:16.091 --> 00:00:22.052
And a link to the city's booking service, which holds the seats for every theatre in town.

8
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.056
The box office needs all four.

9
00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:28.571
And the parts need each other too: the price list needs the seating plan.

10
00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.393
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:35.893
Why not let each class build its own helpers, with new?

12
00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:40.601
Why would anyone write a program whose only job is to build other objects?

13
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:43.358
Let's try it the easy way.

14
00:00:43.358 --> 00:00:47.171
The price list calls new, and gets a seating plan of its own.

15
00:00:47.171 --> 00:00:50.671
So does the seat finder, the part that picks free seats.

16
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:52.572
On the first night, it works.

17
00:00:52.572 --> 00:00:57.805
But now there are two seating plans for one hall, and each one is sure it's the real one.

18
00:00:57.805 --> 00:00:59.569
Nobody knows which to believe.

19
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:08.204
The usual cure is to build everything in one place, near main, and hand each part what it needs, through its constructor.

20
00:01:08.204 --> 00:01:10.443
Java and Kotlin say the same thing.

21
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.087
That block has two jobs.

22
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:17.104
Build in the right order: the settings before the booking client that reads them.

23
00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:20.263
And share the right objects: one plan for everyone.

24
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.328
With six classes, that's easy.

25
00:01:22.328 --> 00:01:28.691
With eighty, someone adds one constructor parameter, and the block stops compiling in three places.

26
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.428
Wouldn't it be nice if something read what each part needs, and did the wiring for us?

27
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:40.267
Picture the theatre's front-of-house manager, an hour before the doors open.

28
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:45.125
Every desk in the foyer has a card that says what it needs before it can open.

29
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:49.935
The box office card says: the booking link, the price list, the sales book.

30
00:01:49.935 --> 00:01:52.735
The price list's card says: the seating plan.

31
00:01:52.735 --> 00:02:01.137
The manager reads every card, sets things out so nothing opens before what it needs, and puts one seating plan on a shelf for everyone.

32
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.116
Let's map that.

33
00:02:03.116 --> 00:02:06.522
The manager is a container, the program we'll build.

34
00:02:06.522 --> 00:02:08.683
Each desk is a part, a component.

35
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.155
Each card is that class's constructor: what it needs.

36
00:02:12.155 --> 00:02:15.692
And the plan on the shelf is a shared part, made once.

37
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.241
So for every desk, a container makes three decisions.

38
00:02:20.241 --> 00:02:21.890
Which classes take part?

39
00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.333
How do you build one?

40
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:24.776
And how many of each?

41
00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:28.492
The smallest container fits in a few lines.

42
00:02:28.492 --> 00:02:34.273
It uses reflection, the JDK's way of asking a class about itself while the program runs.

43
00:02:34.273 --> 00:02:36.966
getConstructors returns the constructors.

44
00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:39.529
getParameterTypes lists what one needs.

45
00:02:39.529 --> 00:02:41.828
And newInstance calls it, like new.

46
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.217
Read it as one sentence: to build a part, build everything its constructor asks for, then call the constructor.

47
00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:51.316
Now a test.

48
00:02:51.316 --> 00:02:57.724
The box office needs a price list and a seat finder, and both of them need a seating plan.

49
00:02:57.724 --> 00:03:03.633
You might hope the JVM notices the same class asked for twice, and reuses the plan.

50
00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:06.196
So, how many seating plans get made?

51
00:03:06.196 --> 00:03:07.335
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:13.266
Here's what the demo printed.

53
00:03:13.266 --> 00:03:17.864
By hand, where we passed one plan twice, new SeatingPlan prints once.

54
00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.929
By reflection, it prints twice.

55
00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:22.556
Well, the JVM is not that thoughtful.

56
00:03:22.556 --> 00:03:25.853
Every newInstance makes a fresh object, like new.

57
00:03:25.853 --> 00:03:30.967
And our few lines have no memory, so every card that asks gets its own plan.

58
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:37.057
So give the manager a shelf: a map from each class to the one object already made.

59
00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:40.602
Look on the shelf first, and build only when it's empty.

60
00:03:40.602 --> 00:03:43.513
Run it again, and new SeatingPlan prints once.

61
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:45.338
Now the names.

62
00:03:45.338 --> 00:03:49.427
A dependency is an object another object needs to do its job.

63
00:03:49.427 --> 00:03:54.186
Injecting it means handing it in from outside, through the constructor.

64
00:03:54.186 --> 00:03:57.604
So our manager is a dependency injection container.

65
00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:02.631
And keeping one object per class, the shelf, is called the singleton scope.

66
00:04:03.533 --> 00:04:06.110
Here's where the manager picture breaks.

67
00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:12.489
A real manager can improvise when a card is unclear, or when cards point at each other in a circle.

68
00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.033
A container that guesses builds a program nobody wrote.

69
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:20.349
So ours reads every card before the doors open, and refuses a loop.

70
00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:25.277
Our few lines still have four flaws, and the lesson fixes each one.

71
00:04:25.277 --> 00:04:27.193
They take the first constructor.

72
00:04:27.193 --> 00:04:29.048
They have no list of the parts.

73
00:04:29.048 --> 00:04:31.742
They would loop forever on cards in a circle.

74
00:04:31.742 --> 00:04:35.154
And the plain shelf breaks when many threads ask at once.

75
00:04:35.154 --> 00:04:38.267
A thread is one path of work, running beside others.

76
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.035
When the doors open, eight customers can reach the box office in the same second.

77
00:04:44.035 --> 00:04:49.407
In the lesson's run, eight threads asked a plain map, and the constructor ran eight times.

78
00:04:49.407 --> 00:04:51.616
Eight customers, eight seating plans.

79
00:04:51.616 --> 00:04:55.078
That's not a theatre any more, that's a chain of theatres.

80
00:04:55.078 --> 00:04:58.540
So the shelf must stay safe when everyone reaches at once.

81
00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:02.116
Over three sittings, the container grows.

82
00:05:02.116 --> 00:05:04.636
This one wires the parts by reflection.

83
00:05:04.636 --> 00:05:08.060
The next one adds stand-ins, to time and retry calls.

84
00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.552
The last one measures the cost, then writes the wiring out before the program starts.

85
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:17.833
So, why a program whose only job is to build objects?

86
00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:21.516
Because the constructors already say what each part needs.

87
00:05:21.516 --> 00:05:23.231
Let one reader follow them.

88
00:05:23.231 --> 00:05:30.280
Build what a card asks for first, keep one of each shared part on the shelf, and refuse any card it can't read.

89
00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:31.204
Quick check.

90
00:05:31.204 --> 00:05:35.936
A ticket printer joins the foyer, and its card asks for the seating plan too.

91
00:05:35.936 --> 00:05:39.745
With the shelf in place, how many seating plans are there now?

92
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:43.656
Still one.

93
00:05:43.656 --> 00:05:47.984
The printer gets the same plan from the shelf, like everyone else.
